WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
It is a heartbreaking reality that in the United States,

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:07.600
drowning is the leading cause of death for children ages one to four.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.520
Drowning is a major emergency that happens when someone's mouth and nose

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.440
are underwater for too long,

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.240
preventing them from breathing. Spotting the signs of drowning early is vital

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.719
because it often doesn't look like the splashing you see in movies.

00:00:20.719 --> 00:00:24.600
Look for someone with their head low in the water, their mouth at water level,

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.639
or someone who is vertical but not using their legs.

00:00:27.639 --> 00:00:31.520
They might be gasping, hyperventilating, or flailing their arms.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.200
Your first priority is safety. Never enter the water if it puts you at risk.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.880
Instead, try to reach out to them with a pole or throw a flotation device.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:45.279
Once the person is out of the water, call 911 immediately and send someone to get

00:00:45.279 --> 00:00:46.599
an AED.

00:00:46.599 --> 00:00:51.320
If the person is unresponsive and not breathing normally, you must start CPR.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:55.520
When starting CPR on a drowning victim, you should always start with five rescue

00:00:55.599 --> 00:01:00.560
breaths before moving into the standard cycle of 30 compressions and two breaths.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.919
Remember, drowning victims need oxygen first. Focus entirely on providing oxygen

00:01:05.919 --> 00:01:09.519
and maintaining circulation. Do not attempt to drain the lungs with

00:01:09.519 --> 00:01:13.320
abdominal thrusts, as this is ineffective and can cause further harm.

